Split GPU thread: acquires met by releases still queued for the back go on without draining it.
(ProsperoEden eglantine-a42b4d4-ps5.d 0009 split-pending-releases, 0013 split-3d-releases,
0014 split-qmd-releases, 0015 split-qmd-values, and the 2 ms throttle of 0023
acquire-memory-check; adapted to EglantinePS5's puller.)

The front remembers the semaphore releases it sent the back (by address space and GPU address,
with the ring position after them): the puller's releases, SEM_EXECUTE releases, and the 3D
engine's payload report releases (Maxwell3D::FrontWrite). An acquire that the last such release
meets, while the back has not run it yet, goes on without draining: the back runs the release
before anything the front sends after the acquire. The front also runs a copy of the QMD
scheduling (the same NativeQmdScheduler on the same QMDs, in a table of its own, dispatching
nothing) to learn which semaphores the back's QMD releases write: plain releases count as exact
values, "add" ones as pending sums on the word's value and "max" ones as lower bounds ("greater or
equal" acquires only); otherwise an acquire on such a word waits only for the back to reach that
release (GpuSplit::DrainTo, a partial drain). The back publishes its progress every 256 words
(was 4096) for those partial waits. A suspended channel's drained check runs at most every 2 ms
(the scheduler polls one thousands of times a second); a latched waiter resumes without it.
EglantinePS5's raw-word check (EDEN_NX2_ACQUIRE_DRAIN_FIRST) and opt-in drain to the last
release (EDEN_NX2_ACQUIRE_DRAIN_TO_RELEASE) stay as they were.

